# Code owners
#
# Automatic review requests. GitHub requests a review from every owner whose
# pattern matches a changed file, so keep this list accurate or it becomes
# noise people learn to dismiss.
#
# Until there is more than one maintainer this file is a placeholder for a real
# review policy. When a second maintainer joins, split the domain patterns
# below and give each person their area. A CODEOWNERS file that routes
# everything to one person is a bottleneck, not a safety net.

# Default: nothing is owned by default, so a new top-level file does not
# silently inherit a review requirement nobody agreed to.
/*                     @SriSatyaLokesh

# The product. A change here changes what an agent says to a contributor.
/plugins/             @SriSatyaLokesh
/skills/              @SriSatyaLokesh

# The mirror. Never hand-edit; it is generated. Owned because a hand-edit here
# fails CI, and the next person needs to know why.
/.agents/             @SriSatyaLokesh

# Release surface.
/.claude-plugin/       @SriSatyaLokesh
/maco.json             @SriSatyaLokesh
/schemas/             @SriSatyaLokesh

# Deterministic tooling. A change to these decides what ships broken and what
# ships blocked, so it gets looked at carefully.
/scripts/             @SriSatyaLokesh
/.github/workflows/   @SriSatyaLokesh

# Docs. Lower risk, but the install instructions are the first thing a
# stranger reads, so a stale README is a real bug.
/docs/                @SriSatyaLokesh
